READING (this is Sections IV-2 and IV-3 of the manuscript, reproduced):
  A returns MISS on a genuinely early alarm, because the onset-anchored rule presumes the
    alarm is still raised at t_onset and a fin transient has already cleared by then.
  B returns a large POSITIVE lead on the same data, because a per-sample FPR does not
    control the alarm-event rate of a trailing-window statistic.
  The two fail in OPPOSITE directions on identical inputs, so the metric choice alone
  spans more than the effect being estimated. C needs no independence assumption.
